On Thu, 15 Oct 2026 10:56:25 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> > +# the upstream patch is relative to the sonic repository
> > +PATCH_WRKSRC=	${WRKSRC_sonic}
>
> I get what's going on here, but I think this is too blunt. I suspect the
> ports framework has a mechanism for patching a specific distfile /
> distdir, whatever. Because otherwise how would you apply a patch to more
> than one level?
>
> Research ports framework, porter's handbook, and other ports to find
> idiomatic technique.

You were right that PATCH_WRKSRC is too broad. It is the directory for
every patch, the PATCHFILES and files/patch-* alike. The framework
has no per-distfile directory. A PATCHFILES entry takes only a strip
level and a site group, patch[:-pX][:group] (Mk/bsd.port.mk, line 112).

What it does have is a separate argument list for distribution
patches. Mk/Scripts/do-patch.sh applies PATCHFILES with
PATCH_DIST_ARGS and files/ patches with PATCH_ARGS. Both run as
"patch -d ${PATCH_WRKSRC} <args>", so a -d in PATCH_DIST_ARGS moves
only the PATCHFILES. net/norm does exactly this for an upstream commit
to its protolib GH_TUPLE:

    PATCHFILES=	ba9ca1ca7d73592268ebfd5a43271303e76f461e.patch:-p1
    PATCH_DIST_ARGS=	-d ../protolib-702e909

editors/nvi-m17n sets -d there too. They are the only two ports that
set PATCH_DIST_ARGS, so this is the framework's knob, not a widespread
habit. The Porter's Handbook does not cover the case.

To your question about more than one level: local patches already
reach any level, since files/patch-* paths are relative to WRKSRC
and can name vendor/... or a tuple's directory. Upstream patches for
several different trees cannot share one -d, and ports that have them
call ${PATCH} -d per tree in post-patch, as editors/textadept does
for scintilla, lua, cdk, and termkey.

v9 [1] drops PATCH_WRKSRC and sets

    PATCH_DIST_ARGS=	-d ${WRKSRC_sonic} --suffix ${DISTORIG} --forward \
    			--quiet -E

net/norm's override drops the default's --forward, --quiet, and -E
along with its -p0. v9 keeps them and takes the strip level from the
entry's :-p1 as before. A clean extract and patch applied the commit
to the sonic tuple, and mmap_unix.go now passes ^uintptr(0) as the
fd. PATCH_WRKSRC is back at WRKSRC, so a later files/patch-* applies
to signoz as usual. The lint tools are clean.

> > +signoz_start_precmd()
> > +{
> > +	if [ ! -e "${pidfile}" ]; then
> > +		install -m 0600 -o "${signoz_user}" -g "${signoz_group}" /dev/null "${pidfile}"
> > +	fi
> > +}
>
> Where does this come from? Can you point to other examples in the framework?

It comes from net-mgmt/prometheus3's rc script, which v1 named as the
model. The function there is the same line for line
(files/prometheus.in, line 136), and prometheus2 has carried it since
5109fb2d069d, "net-mgmt/prometheus2: Update to 2.24.1", in 2021.

The precmd is needed for a reason. With signoz_user set, rc.subr
runs the whole command as that user through "su -m" (/etc/rc.subr,
line 1833), so daemon(8) itself runs as signoz, and "-p" then fails
to create a file in root-owned /var/run. start_precmd still runs as
root, so it creates the pidfile and gives it to signoz first.

It is the tree's common pattern. 191 rc scripts in ports create the
pidfile with install from /dev/null, and 129 of those wrap daemon(8),
e.g. net-mgmt/pushgateway, net-mgmt/mysqld_exporter,
net-mgmt/nfs-exporter, and comms/aprsd. The v9 notes now explain it.

